Grammar
This is what the parser accepts today:
module         := { statement }
statement      := function_def | extern_def | var_decl | return_stmt
                | if_stmt | while_stmt | 'break' ';' | expr_or_assign | ';'
function_def   := 'def' IDENT '(' params ')' [ '->' type ] block
extern_def     := 'extern' 'def' IDENT '(' params ')' [ '->' type ] ';'
params         := [ type IDENT { ',' type IDENT } ]
type           := TYPE_NAME [ '<' type { ',' type } '>' ]
block          := '{' { statement } '}'
var_decl       := [ 'static' ] [ 'const' ] [ type ] IDENT [ '=' expr ] ';'
return_stmt    := 'return' [ expr ] ';'
if_stmt        := 'if' expr block [ 'else' ( if_stmt | block ) ]
while_stmt     := 'while' expr block
expr_or_assign := expr [ ( '=' | '+=' | '-=' | '*=' | '/=' | '%=' ) expr ] ';'

expr           := 'not' expr | unary { binary_op unary }
unary          := ( '-' | '+' | '~' | '++' | '--' ) unary | power
power          := postfix [ '**' unary ]
postfix        := primary { '(' args ')' | 'as' type | '++' | '--' }
args           := [ [ IDENT '=' ] expr { ',' [ IDENT '=' ] expr } ]
primary        := INTEGER | FLOAT | STRING | 'true' | 'false' | IDENT
                | '(' expr ')' | type '(' expr ')'
• TYPE_NAME is one of int i32 i64 float f32 f64 bool str byte void matrix. int is i64, float is f64, byte is an unsigned 8-bit integer.
• Comments start with #, or are /* ... */. // is floor division.
• A def without -> type gets return type auto, inferred by semantic analysis. An extern without one gets void.
• if and while conditions don't need parentheses.
• Nesting deeper than 256 levels (brackets, blocks, prefix and postfix operators, type arguments) is an error, and parsing stops there.
Binary operator precedence, lowest first. All are left-associative except **.
Level
Operators
1
or
2
and
3
not (prefix)
4
==  !=  <  <=  >  >=  is  is not  in  not in
5
|
6
^
7
&
8
<<  >>
9
+  -
10
*  /  //  %  @
11
unary -  +  ~  ++  --
12
** (right-associative)
13
call f(x), cast x as float, postfix ++ --
The lexer recognizes for, import, try, catch, throw, case and none, but the parser rejects them as "not supported yet". Semantic analysis also rejects is, is not, in and not in.
