Semantic rules
• Names must be declared before use; an inner block may shadow an outer name.
• Integers convert to any integer or float type; floats convert to floats.
• / on integers gives a float; // keeps integers.
• a @ b needs two matrices with the same element type (and matching inner dimensions, once shapes are known). A matrix may be multiplied or divided by a number, and added to or subtracted from a matrix of the same type.
• if and while conditions must be bool. break must be inside a loop.
• A def without -> type gets its return type from its return statements. A function that calls itself needs an explicit -> type.
• Arguments may be named (f(x = 1)) after the positional ones.
• Functions can only be defined at the top level.
Team notes: add comments here.
Consumption rules (destructive read)
A matrix variable is consumed when it is an operand of @, an argument of a function call, moved into another variable (b = a), or returned. After that it can't be used until it gets a new value.
• Reading it in m * 2.0, -m or m + n doesn't consume it.
• copy(m) gives a fresh matrix without consuming m.
• A matrix consumed on only one branch of an if, or in an earlier loop iteration, counts as "maybe consumed". Using it is an error too.
Details are in Include/Analysis/destructive.h. These rules are a first version; change them there as the design evolves.
