Benchmark numbers (2026-11-12)
Destructive reads cut peak memory in three of four workloads, by up to 82.4%. Measured with MemoLangTests membench: CPU runtime, 256x256 f32 matrices (0.25 MiB each), Intel Core i7-1185G7, MSVC 19.51 Release. "Base" frees a named matrix when its scope ends or it is reassigned (like C++ / Rust), temporaries at once, and copy() costs nothing. "Destr" frees each matrix when it is consumed.
Workload
Peak memory base → destr (MiB)
Change
Allocations base → destr
named_chain
4.25 → 0.75
-82.4%
17 → 17
layer_loop
1.25 → 0.75
-40.0%
65 → 65
single_expression
2.50 → 2.25
-10.0%
15 → 15
power_iteration
0.75 → 1.00
+33.3%
19 → 35 (+84.2%): the cost of copy()
• Memory numbers are exact and carry over to a GPU. CPU times vary by about ±15% between identical runs, so they don't show a speed difference either way.
• Against native C++ (benchmarks/cpp/, same matrix runtime): plain C++ uses exactly the memory of MemoLang base in all four workloads. C++ with every matrix freed by hand after its last use matches MemoLang destructive in three. The exception is power_iteration, where MemoLang needs copy() and C++ doesn't.
